<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this,
    Solution/src/Other/Solution.xml and package.json in the same commit.
  -->
  <control namespace="PCFHub"
           constructor="RowCommands"
           version="0.1.4"
           display-name-key="RowCommands_Name"
           description-key="RowCommands_Desc"
           control-type="standard">

    <!--
      Nothing here talks to a third party. The URL command hands a string to
      `context.navigation.openUrl` and the platform opens it — the control makes
      no request of its own, so this stays disabled and the component stays
      non-premium.
    -->
    <external-service-usage enabled="false" />

    <!--
      One role, and it is the switch for a whole command.

      `urlField` is optional. When the maker maps a column, the row gets a
      **Launch** button; when they do not, it does not appear. That is why there
      is no `showUrl` input property: the mapping *is* the switch, and a boolean
      beside it would be a second way to say the same thing that can disagree
      with the first.

      **Found by `alias`, read by `name`.** `dataset.columns.find(c => c.alias
      === 'urlField')` gives the column; `record.getValue(column.name)` gives the
      value. Backwards, the find never matches, nothing errors, and the control
      renders no Launch button against a real view — which is how that bug
      reached production in pcf-tag-list. `demo/records.json` deliberately gives
      this column an `alias` that differs from its `name` so the mistake fails
      where it can be seen.

      The type group is not decoration. A URL lives in a `SingleLine.URL` column
      on a table designed for one and in a `SingleLine.Text` column on every
      table that grew one later, and `of-type` restricting to the wrong one of
      those does not fail — it empties the column picker in the configuration
      pane, which reads as a broken control. Both types generate
      `StringProperty`, so this costs no cast; what it may cost is canvas, since
      `of-type-group` is commonly read as model-driven only. That is recorded in
      SPEC.md under *Not verified* rather than assumed either way.

      No `cds-data-set-options`: this control never calls
      `setSelectedRecordIds()`, so the subgrid command bar would be furniture
      with nothing behind it, and the view selector would let a user swap in a
      view whose columns the row commands were not configured for.
    -->
    <type-group name="commandUrl">
      <type>SingleLine.URL</type>
      <type>SingleLine.Text</type>
    </type-group>

    <data-set name="records" display-name-key="Records_DataSet_Name">
      <property-set name="urlField"
                    display-name-key="UrlField_Name"
                    description-key="UrlField_Desc"
                    of-type-group="commandUrl"
                    usage="bound"
                    required="false" />
    </data-set>

    <!--
      Inverted, and that is the fix rather than a naming quirk.

      `refreshTypes` generates `TwoOptionsProperty` with `raw: boolean` — not
      `boolean | null` — so a maker who never touches a checkbox gets `false`,
      and `default-value="true"` does not close the gap. A `showOpen` property
      would therefore ship silently switched off, and the maker would have to
      find a checkbox nobody told them about. Naming it for the *unusual* choice
      puts the platform's `false` on the behaviour almost everybody wants.
    -->
    <property name="hideOpen"
              display-name-key="HideOpen_Name"
              description-key="HideOpen_Desc"
              of-type="TwoOptions"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="false" />

    <!--
      And this one is a `showX` on purpose, which is the same rule read the
      other way. Delete should be off until somebody asks for it, so the
      platform's `false` is already the right answer and inverting the name
      would put a destructive command behind a double negative.

      `default-value="false"` is stated rather than omitted, and it is worth
      knowing that it arrives at the control as the *string* `"false"` — truthy
      — on any host that hands defaults over raw. Every read goes through
      `asBoolean`.
    -->
    <property name="showDelete"
              display-name-key="ShowDelete_Name"
              description-key="ShowDelete_Desc"
              of-type="TwoOptions"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="false" />

    <!--
      **No `default-value`, deliberately, and it is the difference between an
      optional property and one that overrides the host on every install.**

      `dataset.paging.pageSize` is the size the platform is already retrieving
      with: the user's own *Rows per page* personalisation on a main grid, the
      maker's setting on a subgrid, the platform default in canvas. A
      `default-value` here would arrive as a real number from a maker who never
      touched the property, and the control would call `setPageSize` with it —
      silently replacing a setting somebody had deliberately chosen.

      Left unset, `raw` is `null` and the control adopts the host's own size
      without asking for anything. Set, it overrides. A `Whole.None` reads back
      as `number | null`, so the two states are distinguishable — which is
      exactly what a `TwoOptions` cannot do, and the reason the two boolean
      inputs above have to be named around the problem instead.
    -->
    <property name="pageSize"
              display-name-key="PageSize_Name"
              description-key="PageSize_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      Three outputs, and the third is the one that is easy to leave out.

      `invokedRecordId` and `invokedCommand` say what happened. `invokeCount`
      says that it happened *again*: OnChange fires on a change, not on a write,
      so pressing Open twice on the same row is one event and one silence
      without a counter. pcf-action-button found this the hard way with its
      PressCount, and it is the same family as pcf-star-rating's `?? undefined`
      clear.

      All three are notified *before* the platform call, so a canvas app sees
      the press even on a host where the call itself does nothing.
    -->
    <property name="invokedRecordId"
              display-name-key="InvokedRecordId_Name"
              description-key="InvokedRecordId_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="output" />

    <property name="invokedCommand"
              display-name-key="InvokedCommand_Name"
              description-key="InvokedCommand_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="output" />

    <property name="invokeCount"
              display-name-key="InvokeCount_Name"
              description-key="InvokeCount_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="output" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/RowCommands.css" order="1" />
      <!--
        THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE AND 1033 GOES LAST. `pcf-start` picks the .resx
        by manifest order rather than by locale — it reads whichever file is
        listed last and ignores the LCID entirely. The platform itself resolves
        from the user's provisioned language and ignores this order completely,
        so `npm start` is the only thing in the world that reads it.

        ADD A NEW LANGUAGE ABOVE THIS LINE.
      -->
      <resx path="strings/RowCommands.3082.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/RowCommands.1036.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/RowCommands.1031.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/RowCommands.1041.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/RowCommands.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>

    <!--
      One feature, and `required="false"` is the load-bearing half.

      WebAPI is Dataverse-dependent and absent in canvas apps. `required="true"`
      on a host that lacks a feature is **component load failure at runtime** —
      the whole control fails to appear, not the one button — so a control that
      can do something useful without it must declare `false` and feature-detect.
      Here that is exactly the shape: without `context.webAPI` the delete
      command is not rendered, and open and launch carry on.

      Nothing else is declared. `openDatasetItem()` is a method on the dataset
      and needs no feature; neither does `context.navigation` — pcf-lookup-search
      calls `openForm` with no declaration at all. `Utility` is not used: this
      control never resolves entity metadata, because `dataset.getTargetEntityType()`
      already answers the only question it would ask.

      Every entry here is an install-time permission prompt for the customer, so
      the commented-out block the template ships is deleted rather than left in
      place — a commented feature declares nothing, but it invites somebody to
      uncomment the pair rather than the one.
    -->
    <feature-usage>
      <uses-feature name="WebAPI" required="false" />
    </feature-usage>
  </control>
</manifest>
